# Known leaks in libraries the GUI uses, so LeakSanitizer reports only ours. Run with
#   LSAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=gui/lsan.supp
#
# GLFW 3.4.0 (bundled with raylib 6.0): every glfwPostEmptyEvent on Wayland leaks the wl_callback
# from wl_display_sync. Fixed in GLFW master, which adds a listener. the main loop posts rarely.
leak:_glfwPostEmptyEventWayland
# GLFW 3.4.0: a window's fractional-scale object and its viewport (both made with HIGHDPI) are
# never destroyed with the window, 2 x 96 bytes once. Fixed in GLFW master.
leak:_glfwCreateWindowWayland
# GLFW (3.4.0 and master): a new keymap replaces the xkb compose state without unreferencing the
# old one, so each keymap event leaks a compose table. Keymaps arrive at startup and on layout
# changes (and on every Hyprland send_shortcut, which the tests use).
leak:keyboardHandleKeymap
leak:xkb_compose_table_new_from_locale
# Mesa's driver keeps a one-time allocation behind pthread_once. The driver is unloaded before
# LeakSanitizer reports, so it shows as <unknown module> and only the pthread_once frame can be
# matched. Our code never calls pthread_once.
leak:pthread_once
